#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""gloam: an internet radio and music player for the terminal, with a
visualiser based on the animated background of lpx.org.uk.

Plays internet radio (Icecast, SHOUTcast and HLS streams, .pls and .m3u
links) and any local audio file ffmpeg can read, and draws the background's
shader in the terminal.

Requirements: Python 3.8+, numpy and ffmpeg, on macOS or Linux. For sound,
install sounddevice (pip install sounddevice), which also keeps the picture
in step with the sound most closely. Without it, gloam falls back to
ffmpeg's macOS audio output, ffplay, paplay, pw-cat, aplay or SoX's play.
Installing certifi lets it read song titles from https streams on Python
builds that lack system certificates.

    python3 gloam.py https://ice1.somafm.com/groovesalad-128-mp3
    python3 gloam.py https://somafm.com/groovesalad.pls
    python3 gloam.py song.flac other.mp3     several sources: n / b to move between them
    python3 gloam.py stations.m3u            a playlist of stations or files
    python3 gloam.py URL --palette ember --hold
    python3 gloam.py URL --react wild        off, subtle, gentle, normal (default), lively,
                                             strong, wild, extreme, unhinged, or 0 to 10
    python3 gloam.py URL --ahead 180         run the picture 180 ms ahead of the sound
                                             (default 130), if it trails the beat
    python3 gloam.py URL --volume 60
    python3 gloam.py URL --no-sound          the picture only

Display options: --full, --mini [C], --num, --chunky (cell layouts),
--256, --nocolour, --fps, --speed, --size WxH, --hue, --brightness,
--colour (richness, default 0.5) and --white (0 lets over-bright cells go
white, 1 keeps their colour). See --help.

Keys while it runs:
    n / b        next / previous source
    up / down    louder / quieter (also 0 / 9)
    m            mute
    r / R        react more / less
    { / }        picture earlier / later against the sound, 10 ms a press
    p            next palette now
    h            hold the palette / let it change
    + / -        turn the colours round the colour wheel
    [ / ]        darker / brighter
    < / >        colours move slower / faster (also , / .)
    i            show where the sound is going
    q            quit (Ctrl-C works too)
"""
